<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sea of Marmara</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Propontis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sea of Marmara, the ancient Propontis, is an inland sea in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> through the Bosporus to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> through the Dardanelles. It separates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Control of its shores and straits made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, rich, and peoples such as the Bithynians lived along its southern coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara.jpg" alt="Sea of Marmara" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Interiot · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkish_Strait_disambig.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Sea of Marmara lies entirely within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and links the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a> by way of the Bosporus and the Dardanelles, separating the country's European and Asian sides. It covers 11,350 square kilometres, measures roughly 280 by 80 kilometres, and is considered the smallest sea in the world. Its maximum depth is 1,370 metres. The south coast is heavily indented and has the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izmit">İzmit</a>, the Gulf of Gemlik, the Gulf of Bandırma and the Gulf of Erdek.</p><p>The surface water averages about 22 parts per thousand of salt, slightly more than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and about two-thirds that of most oceans. At the bottom it is much more saline, averaging about 38 parts per thousand, like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and this dense water does not rise to the surface.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The sea is named after Marmara Island, the largest island on its south side, which is rich in marble, mármaron in Greek. In classical antiquity it was called the Propontis, from the Greek pro, in front of, and pontos, sea, because the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> sailed through it to reach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. In Greek mythology a storm on the Propontis brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argonauts">Argonauts</a> back to an island they had left, which led to a battle in which either <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> killed King Cyzicus, who had mistaken them for his Pelasgian enemies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Two groups of islands lie in the sea. To the north are the Princes' Islands, made up of the inhabited islands Kınaliada, Burgazada, Heybeliada, Büyükada and Sedef Adası, and several uninhabited ones. They are reached by ferry from both shores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and form part of its conurbation. To the south are the Marmara Islands, with Marmara Island, Avşa, Paşalimanı and Ekinlik inhabited and 17 largely uninhabited islands, among them the prison island of Imralı. They lie within Balıkesir Province and are reached from Tekirdağ or from Erdek.</p><p>The North Anatolian Fault runs under the sea. It has caused several major earthquakes, among them those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izmit">İzmit</a> in August 1999 and Düzce in November 1999. The August earthquake is commonly called the Marmara Earthquake, since its epicentre lay under the sea and the places worst affected, by the shaking and a tsunami, lay along its shores. About one third of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>'s population lives in the basin, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, the largest city of the country, stands on the sea.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sea of Marmara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The colonists, perhaps joined by settlers from other Greek cities, occupied the triangular promontory between the Golden Horn, the Bosporus and the <b>Sea of Marmara</b>, with their acropolis on the site of the later Topkapı Palace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its founder, Mithridates I Ktistes (‘the Founder’), belonged to a noble family of Persian origin that had ruled the Greek city of Kios on the <b>Sea of Marmara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Milesians settled the shores of the Hellespont, the <b>Propontis</b> and the Black Sea, founding Abydos, Cyzicus, Sinope, Histria, Apollonia Pontica, Olbia and Panticapaeum, and these colonies sent grain, fish, timber and slaves in return for wine, oil and manufactured goods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Odrysian rulers also hired Greek soldiers: in 400–399 BCE Seuthes II employed the survivors of Xenophon’s Ten Thousand to subdue tribes near the <b>Propontis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Samos had its own sanctuary of Hera at Naucratis in Egypt, and it founded colonies such as Perinthus on the <b>Propontis</b>, Nagidus and Celenderis in Cilicia and Minoa on Amorgos, while exiles from the island settled Dicaearchia, the later Puteoli, in Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek colonies were founded along their coasts from the 8th century BCE, first on the Aegean, then on the <b>Sea of Marmara</b> and the Black Sea, and Thracians and Greeks lived side by side.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
